Date: Wed, 13 Oct 1993 13:15:32 -0400 (EDT)
From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Inconceivable!

I had a situation where my boss was promoted to VP and hired a team
manager to supervise the team I was on. The new manager, unable to
keep up with the project, made her move by sending him a lengthy note
accusing me of making phone calls on the job, reading when I should be
working, etc. After I calmed down, I did point out to my prev boss
that as the system administrator, it was my job to contact vendors and
to take their calls, etc, and that otherwise this whole discussion
wasn't really on a professional level. The implication was that I knew
my job and my status but my new boss hadn't caught on.
  Anyway, after six long months this boss was finally kicked
downstairs and sideways. Her one accomplishment was that our team had
learned to work together very well to avoid her!
  The correlary to your situation is that any manager who becomes
obsessed with trivia instead of focusing on the bottom line is
probably just scared and losing it. Every manager takes an occasional
stab at something that isn't really a problem, and many of us issue an
occasional piece of bluster just to show that we're paying attention.
Also, sometimes this sort of warning comes in response to some higher
pressure. If, say, the President writes a memo to the VP's about
company resources being used for company work, there may be a
trickle-down of warning shots fired at random at the group level. Same
thing happens with copy machines :-P
   If your manager shows a continuing focus on trivia, do take a look
at their position in the company and your team's position...and it IS
trivia, since it neither adds to nor subtracts from company profits or
group productivity (if it is isolated) and doesn't affect public
relations or customer relations. It's no more relevant than a poster
put on the employee bulletin board in the cafeteria.


