Simon! writes:

> of race and culture.  As I wrote in an earlier posting, I am unaware of any
> good introduction to the field in English (most of the scholarship is in
> German and French), but I do recommend _The American Heritage Dictionary of
> Indo-European Roots_, edited by the aforementioned C. Watkins, which contains
> a brief introduction and is full of interesting etymologies that you won't
> find in a typical English dictionary.

This _American Heritage Dictionary of Indo-European Roots_ is
available in a nice paperback for around $10-12, in the dictionary
section of your bookstore. And I recommend the new Third Edition of
the American Heritage Dictionary, which is a nearly-unabridged
dictionary ("unabridged" apparently having some technical meaning
which the Am Her does not quite meet) that is nicely laid-out, with
excellent definitions, lots of pictures (a no-no for the OED folks,
but very useful for the rest of us), and a condensd Indo-European
roots section.

I've written before about my fascination with IE roots. In fact, I've
been thinking that learning a few hundred IE roots would be
outstanding training for later seeing commonalities between the major
Western languages (and also Sanskrit and its derivative languages,
like Telegu).

Flip though the IE Roots book and you'll see what I mean. 


-Tim May


