Let me underscore this point, as it was just driven home to me in a
personal defense course I am taking. The use of deadly force is
justified when you believe it to be the only alternative that will
prevent death or grievous bodily harm coming to you or to an innocent
third party.

When you use a weapon in such a situation, the objective is to
neutralize the threat, not to kill the offender. If drawing your weapon
is enough, that's where you stop. If you have to shoot and that stops
the attack, you've reached your objective, even if the shot doesn't hit
the target.  If only the offender's death stops the attack, well it's
regrettable but you *were* trying to prevent an innocent coming to
harm, right? (Most of the time, by far, just presenting the weapon and
being ready to use it will terminate the encounter. The NRA instructors
giving the course also claimed that only about 3% of the people who
suffer gunshot wounds, including self-inflicted wounds in attempted
suicides, actually die.)

When you have to answer for your actions in court, what the court will
want to know is: did you use lethal force only because you had no other
option and did you stop when the threat was gone?

One of the questions I will be bringing up when we get specifically
into the section of the course that deals with the legal aspects of all
this is whether a person breaking into your house is assumed to
represent a threat to life and limb.

