onomoto (or Young and Loud, I can't figure out which name "hir"
prefers):

> 	Um.. granted most of my information is from popular media
> (I'm working on fixing that), but most of what I've read on the
> subject of vests gives me the impression that while wearing a vest
> you still take a fair amount of damage from a bullet.  Stuff like
> broken ribs, etc..
> 	The bullet's energy has to go somewhere, no?

Sure, but there are two factors to consider:

1. Kinetic energy of the bullet (.5 mv^2). This is usually measured in
foot-pounds, as in "250 foot-pounds" (typical for a .38). In
principle, this could all be converted to heat/vibration/sound/light
and do no underlying harm to the vest wearer.

2. Momentum (mv). Popularly called "knockdown powere," this can be no
greater than that felt by the shooter. That is, the mv of the
gun/hand/arm/shooter will be no less than than that felt by the
target. And people shoot large guns all the time.

So, a properly built vest could absorb the incoming round without any
damage. (With padding, plates, whatever.)

In practice, vests have certain constraints on padding, size, weight,
etc., and tradeoffs are made. A bruised chest area is considered a
good tradeoff to allow the vest to be cooler, lighter, less bulky,
more concealable, etc. After all, most cops will never be shot at all,
and so this bruise will happen infrequently. Broken ribs are even less
common, but are still a reasonable risk to take.

(Being shot frequently would of course demand more protection, but
this will not happen in the real world.)

